# A model path that is absolute on one platform and relative on the other

`resolve_model_path` is the single funnel through which a model-supplied path reaches both the
permission decision and the filesystem, for Read, Write, Edit, search_replace, glob, the shell tool's
creation paths, and `xai-grok-workspace`'s `edit_target_protection`. Two of its Windows behaviours were
recorded on 2026-10-03 as identified-but-unfixed, because the product question behind them had not been
answered. This file records the answer, the code that follows from it, what could be proven on a Linux
host, and what could not.

== 1. What was recorded, and the code that produced it

The row in `TODO.md` (written in Chinese, as that file is) claimed two Windows-only defects, both
reachable only through one branch: that the "the model dropped the leading slash" recovery branch
fabricates `format!("/{}", expanded)`, which on Windows conjures a path on the current drive that is
neither cwd-relative nor where the user meant; and that `cwd.join(input_path)`, given a drive-less
rooted argument like `\home\user\...`, drops the whole cwd per `PathBuf::push` semantics.

Both stop at the same question: on Windows, is a path that looks absolute but carries no drive
absolute (resolve against the current drive) or relative (resolve against cwd)? The code, before:

    if !input_path.is_absolute() && !expanded.is_empty() {
        let as_absolute = std::path::PathBuf::from(format!("/{}", expanded.as_ref()));
        let effective_base = display_cwd.unwrap_or(cwd);
        if as_absolute.starts_with(effective_base)
            && let Ok(suffix) = as_absolute.strip_prefix(effective_base)
        {
            return crate::util::fs::join_relative(cwd, suffix);
        }
    }
    cwd.join(input_path)

== 2. What Windows actually does with these two shapes, from std itself

No Windows machine was needed for this part: `std::path` documents and implements it in one place. The
pinned toolchain's own source, `library/std/src/path.rs`, on `PathBuf::push`:

      On Windows:
      * if `path` has a root but no prefix (e.g., `\windows`), it
        replaces everything except for the prefix (if any) of `self`.
      * if `path` has a prefix but no root, it replaces `self`.

and in the body of `_push`, the branch that implements the first bullet, with std's own comment:

      // `path` has a root but no prefix, e.g., `\windows` (Windows only)
      } else if path.has_root() {
          let prefix_len = self.components().prefix_remaining();
          self.inner.truncate(prefix_len);

`Path::has_root` settles which spellings land in that branch: on Windows a path has a root if it has no
prefix and begins with a separator (`\windows`), or has a prefix followed by a separator (`c:\windows`
but not `c:windows`), or has any non-disk prefix (`\\server\share`). `is_absolute` is the stricter
question, and it is the one every caller asks. The gap between the two is the defect:

    spelling                     has_root  prefix  is_absolute   push's rule
    \src\main.rs                 yes       none    false         keep drive, drop cwd path
    /src/main.rs                 yes       none    false         keep drive, drop cwd path
    C:work\plan.md               no        C:      false         replace the whole base
    C:\work\plan.md              yes       C:      true          replace the whole base
    \\server\share\plan.md       yes       UNC     true          replace the whole base

The raw bytes that branch leaves are `<prefix>` concatenated with the argument: `prefix_remaining` is the
length of the `Prefix` component alone, and the separator after a drive letter parses as part of the
following `RootDir`, so truncating to the prefix leaves `D:` and the argument's own separator is the only
one. `D:\worktree\abc` plus `\src\main.rs` is therefore `D:\src\main.rs`. Section 8 prints this from a
Windows binary rather than from reading `_push`; the first draft of this log recorded a doubled separator
here, which is what reading the truncate without running it produced.

The row's second claim is therefore exactly right. Its first is right that a `/`-prefixed candidate is
fabricated and wrong about what it does with it: `as_absolute` is only ever compared against a base and
never returned, so on Windows the recovery branch conjured nothing and simply never ran. That is a third
defect, in section 4, rather than the one that was described. From a cwd of `D:\worktree\abc`, an
argument of `\src\main.rs` produced `D:\src\main.rs`: outside the workspace, reached from an argument
that every caller still classifies as relative.

== 3. The decision

A model path that this platform does not consider absolute is resolved inside the announced cwd, after
the `display_cwd` fold has had its chance. Nothing is resolved against the drive root, and nothing is
resolved against the process's current directory on some drive.

- The drive-root reading cannot be what a model means. `\src\main.rs` is what a transcript produces
  when a path crosses between POSIX-shaped history and a Windows cwd, or when a tool result rendered
  with `/` separators lost its front; in neither case did anyone intend the drive root.
- The result of this function is used twice, for the permission decision and for the I/O, so folding
  cannot smuggle an edit past `edit_target_protection`: protection is evaluated on the folded path, and
  the folded path is the path that gets opened. The drive-root reading has the opposite property, it
  moves the file out from under a decision that was already made.
- The `display_cwd` fold is the existing precedent for the same instinct: a path from conversation
  history is rewritten onto the real worktree rather than taken literally.
- The recovery branch already existed to undo a dropped front. It just did not work where it was needed.

Concretely, for a cwd of `D:\worktree\abc` and a display cwd of `C:\home\user\project`:

    input                                  before                  now
    \src\main.rs                           D:\src\main.rs          D:\worktree\abc\src\main.rs
    /src/main.rs                           D:\src\main.rs          D:\worktree\abc\src\main.rs
    \home\user\project\src\main.rs         D:\home\user\...        D:\worktree\abc\src\main.rs
    home\user\project\src\main.rs          doubled path            D:\worktree\abc\src\main.rs
    C:work\plan.md                         C:work\plan.md          D:\worktree\abc\work\plan.md
    C:\etc\hosts                           unchanged               unchanged
    \\fileserver\share\plan.md             unchanged               unchanged
    src\main.rs                            unchanged               unchanged

Both columns are printed by a Windows binary in section 8: the `before` column is section 8's table 2,
which is the `cwd.join(input)` tail the old code fell through to, and the `now` column is its tables 3, 5
and 6, which run the shipped component match and the shipped component-by-component join. The rows whose
`before` value is not in that printout are the last three, which neither branch touches.

== 4. The third defect, which the same branch hid

The `format!("/{}", input)` candidate could not fail to match on a POSIX base and could not succeed on a
Windows one: a path built as `/\home\user\project\src\main.rs` carries no `C:` prefix, so
`starts_with(display_cwd)` was false against every Windows `display_cwd`. The recovery branch, which
three tests in `types/resources.rs` exist to protect, was dead code on Windows. That is why the doubled
path those tests were written against:

    $ grep -n "forgot_leading_slash" crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/types/resources.rs | head
    resolve_model_path_forgot_leading_slash_exact_cwd
    resolve_model_path_forgot_leading_slash_subpath
    resolve_model_path_forgot_leading_slash_with_display

...was the live Windows behaviour all along: `home\user\project\src\main.rs` against a cwd of
`D:\worktree\abc` produced `D:\worktree\abc\home\user\project\src\main.rs`.

The replacement compares components instead of rebuilding a candidate, so it needs no invented path and
works the same on both platforms. `recover_dropped_root(body, base)` matches `base`'s components below
its own root off the front of `body` and returns what is left; the two spellings that reach it (the
separator dropped, or the separator and the drive together) are then the same question.

One deliberate difference came out of the component rewrite. The old branch prepended `/` to the raw
string *before* parsing, which silently deleted a leading `.`: an input of `./home/user/project/x` was
folded as if it had been `home/user/project/x`. A path that starts with `./` is relative on purpose, so
it is now left where the model put it, pinned by
`resolve_model_path_explicitly_relative_dot_is_not_folded`.

Three existing tests had to be re-spelled rather than re-expected, because their literals were POSIX
shapings of "an absolute path somewhere else": `resolve_model_path_absolute_non_matching`,
`resolve_model_path_partial_prefix_no_match`, `resolve_model_path_sensitive_edit_spellings`. On Windows
their `/etc/hosts` is the drive-less case, so it is now folded into the cwd; each now builds its fixture
with the `root()`/`root_str()` helpers that re-root a path onto `C:\` there, which keeps the intent (an
absolute path outside the announced cwd passes through untouched) true on both platforms. No assertion
about behaviour was weakened: on Linux the helpers are the identity, and those three tests assert the
same strings they did before.

== 5. What a Linux host can reach, and what it cannot

On Linux every leading-separator path is absolute, so the new `rooted_without_drive` branch is
unreachable from any input. That is not an opinion about coverage, it is visible in the parse. The probe
of section 8, compiled for this host instead, prints:

    $ ./model-path-windows-probe | head -12
    platform: linux

    == 1. is_absolute, and what the components look like ==
    input                              absolute     components
    C:\work\proj                       false        Normal(C:\work\proj)
    \src\main.rs                       false        Normal(\src\main.rs)
    /src/main.rs                       true         RootDir Normal(src) Normal(main.rs)
    \\fileserver\share\plan.md         false        Normal(\\fileserver\share\plan.md)
    C:work\plan.md                     false        Normal(C:work\plan.md)

`Normal(\src\main.rs)` is the whole story: on Linux the backslash is an ordinary character, so there is
no root, no prefix, and no way to express the Windows question at all. What Linux can prove is the
component matching, the two helpers, and the platform-neutral parts of the decision; the five
`#[cfg(windows)]` tests are the only thing that pins the Windows branch itself, and section 8 runs them on
a Windows target rather than leaving them compiled out.

== 6. The mutation batch, on both targets, including the one that escaped on Linux

Each mutation is injected into the real file, the module is run, and the file is restored and `cmp`-ed.
Baseline and post-restore runs: `62 passed; 0 failed`.

    MA  recover_dropped_root always declines
        RED, 4 failures: the three forgot_leading_slash tests and the helper test
    MB  a base with nothing below its root declines
        RED, 1 failure: recover_dropped_root_matches_whole_components_from_the_start
    MC  any component counts as a match (equality guard dropped)
        RED, 14 failures, including dotdot_relative, colon_prefixed_display_path,
        interior_quotes_preserved and the eight other relative-path pins
    MD  rooted_without_drive never matches
        RED, 1 failure: rooted_without_drive_only_matches_a_bare_leading_root
    ME  resolve_model_path stops stripping the leading root
        GREEN, 62 passed  <- escaped

ME is not a hole in the batch, it is the result section 5 predicts: dropping the drive-less handling
outright changes no Linux-observable behaviour, because no Linux input can reach it. ME is exactly the
mutation that `resolve_model_path_rooted_without_a_drive_stays_inside_cwd` and
`resolve_model_path_drive_less_display_path_still_folds_to_the_worktree` exist to catch, and those run only
on Windows.

Recording that escape was the point of the section, and it did not stay an escape. The same batch was run
again through the container of section 8, so the module under mutation is a Windows build executed by
wine; baseline `67 passed; 0 failed`, restored byte-identically, final run `67 passed; 0 failed`:

    mutation                                  Linux      Windows
    MA recover_dropped_root always declines   4 failed   9 failed
    MB a base with nothing below its root     1 failed   1 failed
    MC any component counts as a match        14 failed  19 failed
    MD rooted_without_drive never matches     1 failed   4 failed
    ME stops stripping the leading root       escaped    3 failed

ME's three Windows failures are `resolve_model_path_drive_less_display_path_still_folds_to_the_worktree`,
`resolve_model_path_quoted_literal_backslash_n` and `resolve_model_path_trailing_newline`. The first is the
test written for it; the other two are pre-existing POSIX-shaped tests that were already exercising the
fold without being about it, which is worth knowing about the suite as well. The boundary section 5 drew
for the Linux suite is therefore closed by execution rather than argued around: the mutation that escaped
there is caught on the platform the branch belongs to.

== 7. The consumer that decides whether this is a security change

`xai-grok-workspace`'s permission manager calls `resolve_model_path` before
`edit_target_protection`, for `AccessKind::Edit` and for the creation paths of a `Bash` access that
changes directory. 666 permission tests pass unchanged:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -p xai-grok-workspace --lib permission::
    test result: ok. 666 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 1269 filtered out

and the fixtures in that path use `tempfile` directories, i.e. absolute cwds, which is why none of them
moves. The property that matters is the one in section 3: because protection is evaluated on this
function's output and the same output is handed to the I/O, folding a drive-less path into the cwd
cannot make a protected file reachable that was reachable before. It can only make a path that would
have failed at the drive root instead hit a real file inside the workspace, which is the behaviour the
`display_cwd` fold has always had for forked sessions.

== 8. Executed on a Windows binary

Everything above is a claim about Windows, so it was executed there instead of argued from
documentation. `docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe.rs` calls the same `std::path` API the shipped
code uses, plus verbatim transcriptions of `crate::util::fs::join_relative` and of the component match in
`recover_dropped_root`, and is compiled twice: once for this host, once for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`.
`docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe.Dockerfile` builds the cross image, and
`docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe-shim.c` is explained below.

    $ docker build -t chaos-winprobe:local -f docs/verification/model-path-windows-probe.Dockerfile .
    Need to get 214 MB of archives
    ...
    rustc 1.94.0 (4a4ef493e 2026-03-02)
    x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc (GCC) 12-win32
    wine-8.0

Three environment facts cost an attempt each and are worth recording:

- Debian bookworm's `wine64` installs no `wine` wrapper on `PATH`. The entry point is
  `/usr/lib/wine/wine64`, which `dpkg -L wine64` shows; a build step that ran
  `command -v wine || command -v wine64` died on exit 127 for exactly this reason.
- Every binary rustc produces for `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` imports one function from a DLL wine 8.0 does
  not ship:

      $ x86_64-w64-mingw32-objdump -x probe.exe | grep -A3 "DLL Name: bcryptprimitives"
      DLL Name: bcryptprimitives.dll
      vma:  Hint/Ord Member-Name Bound-To
      d18e8    1  ProcessPrng

  Running one anyway exits 53, which is 0xC0000135 STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND truncated, and `fn main() {}`
  compiled the same way fails identically, so nothing Rust-produced can load. A trivial C program from
  the same mingw runs fine (exit 7), which localises the gap to that single import:
  `bcryptprimitives.dll` is the Windows 10 split of `bcrypt.dll` and wine grew it after 8.0.

- That gap was closed with the shim file above, a mingw-built DLL exporting `ProcessPrng` that prints one
  line to stderr on every call:

      $ x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -shared -o bcryptprimitives.dll model-path-windows-probe-shim.c
      $ cd "$(dirname probe.exe)" && /usr/lib/wine/wine64 probe.exe
      wine exit: 0

  The run's stderr holds no `SHIM ProcessPrng called` line, so the shim was loaded and never invoked. It
  is a loader requirement, not part of what is measured: every row below comes out of std's own Windows
  parser.

The tables are the Windows run, one per question. Table 1 is what the platform thinks each spelling is:

