The same container runs the shipped module's own tests, not only a transcription of two loops. The crate
that stopped the `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` route is not an obstacle for the gnu target once it is handed a
Windows C toolchain:

    $ cargo build -p aws-lc-sys --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu
    Compiling aws-lc-sys v0.39.1
    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 12.68s

With `CARGO_TARGET_X86_64_PC_WINDOWS_GNU_LINKER=x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc` plus a `CARGO_TARGET_..._RUNNER`
that copies the shim beside the executable and execs `/usr/lib/wine/wine64`, the real test binary runs:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib types::resources:: --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu
    test result: ok. 67 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3030 filtered out

67 against Linux's 62, the difference being exactly the five `#[cfg(windows)]` tests, which execute here
for the first time. Two caveats stay in the open. The harness does reach the shim, one
`SHIM ProcessPrng called with 16 bytes` line per test where libtest seeds a hasher, which is why the shim
forwards to wine's own `BCryptGenRandom` instead of filling the buffer itself; no test reads randomness.
And a wine prefix is not Windows: it is Debian's wine 8.0, so anything depending on real NTFS semantics,
real access control, or a real per-process drive state remains CI's `platform tests (windows-latest)` leg
to decide. The path arithmetic in this row depends on none of those.

== 9. The neighbouring assertion that pinned the separator it was meant to ignore

`resolve_plan_file_path`, in the same file as the code above, builds the plan file's model-visible path with
`crate::util::fs::join_posix_relative`, component-wise, because `PLAN_FILE_RELATIVE_PATH` carries `/` and a
plain `join` on Windows yields `C:\proj\.grok/plan.md`. That shipped in the previous round, and the Windows
leg of CI turned red over the tests rather than the code:

    run 37118762740  head d0313952  windows-latest  cargo test (target-OS crates: xai-grok-tools)
      3043 passed; 44 failed
    run 37108083983  head dc389877  windows-latest  same step
      3006 passed; 79 failed

Diffing the failing test names is what makes those two numbers readable: 39 fixed, 3 newly broken, all three
in `implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode::tests` (`exit_with_plan_content`,
`prompt_format_includes_plan_content`, `sends_plan_mode_exited_notification_with_content`).

Each asserted `plan_file_path.ends_with(".grok/plan.md")`. On Linux the two separators are the same
character, so that assertion could never tell the location apart from the spelling; on Windows it failed
precisely when the display path started being assembled correctly. It was pinning the mixed-separator
string that `join_posix_relative` exists to prevent. The compared string is now built for the target:

    crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grok_build/exit_plan_mode/mod.rs

      fn plan_file_suffix() -> String {
          std::path::Path::new(".grok").join("plan.md").to_string_lossy().into_owned()
      }

Whether the re-spelled assertions still guard the production call site was settled by one mutation, run on
both targets. `MF` puts the plain join back:

    -        crate::util::fs::join_posix_relative(&cwd.0, PLAN_FILE_RELATIVE_PATH)
    +        cwd.0.join(PLAN_FILE_RELATIVE_PATH)

    filter: implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode

      target                    baseline            MF                after restore
      linux (host)              17 passed; 0 failed GREEN (escaped)   17 passed; 0 failed
      x86_64-pc-windows-gnu     17 passed; 0 failed RED, 3 failed     17 passed; 0 failed

`restored byte-identically: True` on both. The Linux escape is the finding rather than a gap: `/` is the
separator there, so `join` and the component-wise join cannot differ, which means an assertion that
tolerates `MF` on Linux and fails on it on Windows is guarding the fix and nothing else. The three names
below are the three names the CI run reported, which is where the wine run stops being a paraphrase of the
CI record:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode \
          --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu
    test implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode::tests::exit_with_plan_content ... FAILED
    test implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode::tests::prompt_format_includes_plan_content ... FAILED
    test implementations::grok_build::exit_plan_mode::tests::sends_plan_mode_exited_notification_with_content ... FAILED
    test result: FAILED. 14 passed; 3 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3080 filtered out

One caution about reading CI in general: runs `37114697856` and `37110356858` have the platform job
`skipped`, so their green steps are the absence of a measurement, not a result.

== 10. Left open on purpose

- `recover_dropped_root` matches case-sensitively, like the `strip_prefix` fold it replaces. Windows
  path comparison is case-insensitive, so `HOME\User\Project` against a display cwd of
  `C:\home\user\project` is not folded. Making it case-insensitive is a decision about the fold as a
  whole, not about this branch, and it would change forked-session behaviour for POSIX-shaped histories.
- A drive-less path that does not match the display cwd is joined onto the cwd verbatim, which can land
  on a file that exists (`\src\a.rs` becoming `D:\worktree\abc\src\a.rs`). That is intended, it is the
  recovery, but it means a model that once typed a drive-less path can now reach a workspace file
  instead of failing at the drive root. Nothing in the permission layer distinguishes those two.
- The `C:`-relative spelling (`C:work\plan.md`) is folded by dropping the drive. An alternative is to
  refuse it as ambiguous. It has never been observed in a transcript, and the row it came from did not
  name it, so it got the uniform rule rather than its own.
